<!--
TEMPLATE: templates/pages/topic-hub.md.template
RENDERS TO: wiki/topics/<slug>.md — one file per theme
TOKENS: {{ENTRY_NOUN_PLURAL}}
Skeleton for hand-creating a topic hub. Hubs are always written and maintained by the librarian
(Phase 5), never by a sub-agent, so this skeleton is the working template rather than a fallback.
Copy the file, then delete this comment block — the `---` must be the first line of the finished
page, or the frontmatter will not parse.
-->
---
title: "[Topic name in title case]"
tags: [tag-one, tag-two]
entries: ["[[entry-one]]", "[[entry-two]]", "[[entry-three]]"]
sources: ["[[source-one]]", "[[source-two]]"]
---

# [Topic name — same wording as the title field]

[2–4 sentences stating the claim this hub makes about the topic. Not "this page collects entries
about X" — say what the topic is actually about and why the pieces belong together.]

## [First move in the argument, named for the move and not for an entry]

[Prose. Introduce [[entry-one]] on first mention and say what job it does in the argument, then
carry the thread into the next idea. The reader should be able to follow this section without
opening a single link.]

## [Second move — the mechanism, the failure mode, or the counter-position]

[More prose, wiring in [[entry-two]] and [[entry-three]]. Where two entries genuinely disagree,
say so and leave the tension open; a hub that silently picks a winner destroys information.]

## [How the pieces fit together]

[Short closing section that traces the loop: which entry leads to which, and what a reader should
do with the topic as a whole.]

## See also

- [[sibling-topic]] — [what that hub covers that this one deliberately leaves out]

<!--
RULES FOR THIS PAGE TYPE

- A hub is an argument, not a link list. A page that only lists links adds nothing over
  `wiki/index.md` and should not exist.
- Every slug in `entries:` must appear somewhere in the body prose, and every entry wikilinked in
  the body must appear in `entries:`. Adding a slug to the frontmatter is not integration.
- Phase 5 of every ingest wires the batch's new entries into the prose of the hubs they belong to.
  If an entry cannot be woven into any existing argument, that is the signal to consider a new hub.
- Name `##` sections after the move they make in the argument, not after the entries they contain.
- Hubs link sideways to other hubs under `## See also`; do not turn that section into a second
  entry list.
- Create a new hub when roughly five or more {{ENTRY_NOUN_PLURAL}} cluster with no home. The
  reference implementation runs 12 hubs across 328 entries — hubs are few and load-bearing.
- Field name note: this kit uses `entries:`. The reference implementation's files say `concepts:`
  because its entry noun is "concept"; pick one spelling and never mix them.
- Hubs are the one page type that may grow long. Prune and rewrite rather than appending forever.

Full schema reference: docs/03-page-schemas.md. Worked example: examples/example-topic-hub.md.
-->
